#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    // Step 1: Declare and initialize the array
    double sales[] = {12.25, 32.50, 16.90, 23.00, 45.68};
    int size = 5;
    double sum = 0, average = 0;

    cout << "Sales values: ";
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        cout << sales[i] << " ";
        sum += sales[i];
    }

    average = sum / size;
    cout << "\nAverage sales: " << average << endl;

    return 0;
}


Sales values: 12.25 32.5 16.9 23 45.68 
Average sales: 26.866

Step 2: Modify the Initialization

If you change your array to only three values like this:

double sales[5] = {12.25, 32.50, 16.90};

The last two elements will automatically be set to 0.0 because they are uninitialized numeric elements in a fixed-size array.

Step 3: Observe What Happens if You Specify a Smaller Size

If you write something like:

double sales[3] = {12.25, 32.50, 16.90, 23.00, 45.68};

This will cause a compilation error, because there are more initial values than the declared size. C++ requires that the number of elements doesn’t exceed the array’s declared capacity.

1. What values are assigned to the uninitialized elements?
When you declare an array with a fixed size but provide fewer values, C++ automatically fills the remaining elements with zero (0 or 0.0).
For example, if you declare an array of size 5 but only assign 3 values, the last two elements become 0.0 by default.

2. What happens if you specify a size smaller than the number of initial values?
If you declare a smaller size than the number of values you’re trying to assign, the program won’t compile. C++ will show an “excess elements in array initializer” error. This happens because the array doesn’t have enough space to store all the values you listed.
